    "last_attempts_log": [
      {
        "attempt": 1,
        "status": "OK",
        "duration_seconds": 62.251,
        "asr_text": "Imagine sitting down to read. Your phone is face down. It makes no sound. You do not reach for it. It seems absent from the moment. But concentration can be affected in two different contexts: when an alert is presented and when the phone is nearby, even when no notifications are received. In a 2022 study, 73 university students did a visual letter task. Some trials had a phone notification sound. Others had a neutral sound. The students did not look at or touch a phone, yet they responded significantly slower after the notification sound. A second study in 2023 looked at the quiet time before any alert. 42 university students took a concentration test with a phone nearby and with it in another room. The phone was off and no notification came. Attention and processing speed were both lower when the phone was on the desk.",
